<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eric the Victorious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 995</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Eric VI of Sweden</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eric the Victorious was a king of the Swedes around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> in the late 10th century, the first Swedish king known with reasonable certainty. He won his name by defeating his nephew Styrbjörn the Strong at Fýrisvellir near Uppsala in the 980s. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a>, the first Christian king, ruled both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious.jpg" alt="Eric the Victorious" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MELLIN(1850)_p1.264_ERIK_SEGERSALL.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
